{"term":"jargon","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:c5d7b5f9d12abe049a","definition":"A technical terminology unique to a particular subject.","example":"He used technical jargon but I managed to catch the drift of his argument.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["vernacular","jargon","language","parlance","phraseology","sublanguage","terminology"],"antonyms":["vernacular"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"uncountable","wordFamily":["blargon","cyberjargon","Eurojargon","jargonal","jargon aphasia","jargonaphasia","jargonaut","jargoneer","jargoner","jargonese","jargonesque","jargonic","jargonisation","jargonise","jargonish","jargonist","jargonistic","jargonitis","jargonium","jargonization","jargonize","jargonless","jargony","psychojargon","technojargon"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jargon#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:08bf0055d9a81686df","definition":"A language characteristic of a particular group.","example":"They [the Normans] abandoned their native speech, and adopted the French tongue, in which Latin was the predominant element. They speedily raised their new language to a dignity and importance which it had never before possessed. They found it a barbarous jargon; they fixed it in writing; and they employed it in legislation, in poetry, and in romance.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["argot","cant","intalk","lingo"],"antonyms":["vernacular"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable","wordFamily":["blargon","cyberjargon","Eurojargon","jargonal","jargon aphasia","jargonaphasia","jargonaut","jargoneer","jargoner","jargonese","jargonesque","jargonic","jargonisation","jargonise","jargonish","jargonist","jargonistic","jargonitis","jargonium","jargonization","jargonize","jargonless","jargony","psychojargon","technojargon"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.or